        ":question/id": "7c1117fb-96ed-5032-8c50-48772b44cf5c",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113668",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{13}$ and ${F}^{\u2032}(x)=f(x).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{\u2032}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{\u2032}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{\u2032}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=13$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{13}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{13+1}}{13+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{14}}{14})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{14}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{14})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{14}\\cdot 14\\,{x}^{13} & = \\\\ {x}^{13}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
